   "latency_s": 12.965,
   "content": "### Imputation Rules for City Values in Restaurant Records:\n\n1. **Use Specific, Commonly Recognized City Names**: Use the full name of the city without abbreviations (e.g., \"Las Vegas\" not \"LV\").\n\n2. **City Granularity**:\n   - For well-known cities like New York, use \"New York\" or \"New York City\" based on the context.\n   - For smaller areas or neighborhoods, use the broader city name (e.g., \"Marina del Rey\" is part of Los Angeles but should be used as is).\n\n3. **Address Cues**:\n   - **State Abbreviations**: If an address includes a state abbreviation (e.g., \"NY\"), use it to infer the city (e.g., \"New York\").\n   - **Street Names and Numbers**: Use street names and numbers to identify cities (e.g., \"3400 Las Vegas Blvd. S\" implies \"Las Vegas\").\n\n4. **Phone Number Area Codes**:\n   - **212, 917, 347, 646, 718, 929**: New York or New York City.\n   - **415, 628**: San Francisco.\n   - **702**: Las Vegas.\n   - **310, 424**: Los Angeles area (including Marina del Rey and West LA).\n   - **404, 678, 470**: Atlanta.\n\n5. **Type of Restaurant**:\n   - While the type of restaurant can sometimes hint at a city\u2019s cuisine, it is not a reliable sole indicator. Use it in conjunction with other cues.\n\n6. **Special Cases**:\n   - \"New York City\" should be used for addresses on well-known streets like \"W. 46th St.\" or \"1 W. 67th St.\"\n   - For ambiguous cases, default to the broader city name (e.g., \"Los Angeles\" instead of a specific neighborhood).\n\n7. **Consistency**:\n   - Ensure consistency with existing records for the same address or phone number.\n\nBy following these rules, the assistant can accurately impute the missing city values in the dataset.",
